From: OpenAI-assisted development
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] firstboot: stop relying on an invalid UCI delete as control flow

99-dir842-m5 intentionally used `delete wireless` because old uci rejects a
package-only delete, making it a silent no-op.  radio1 survived only because the
command was invalid.

Make the intent explicit and future-proof: reset only the 5 GHz sections this
script owns (radio0/default_radio0), and leave radio1 untouched.  This preserves
the dual-band first-boot behavior without depending on an error in a command
whose semantics could change in another UCI version/backport.

--- a/files/target/linux/realtek/base-files/etc/uci-defaults/99-dir842-m5
+++ b/files/target/linux/realtek/base-files/etc/uci-defaults/99-dir842-m5
@@ -141,14 +141,12 @@ case "$DIR842_BASE_MAC" in
 # --- 5 GHz AP on the PCIe RTL8822BE (rtw88); 2x2, HT20 (see below). hwmode 11a = 5 GHz on
 #     this 4.14-era netifd. country BR unlocks the UNII-1 channels for beaconing
 #     (world/'00' marks them no-IR). macaddr set because the efuse is blank. ---
-# ⚠ The batch below opens with `delete wireless` — KEEP AS-IS, it is a NO-OP and
-#   that is load-bearing. Whole-config `uci delete wireless` (no section) is an
-#   INVALID statement: "uci: Invalid argument", exit 1, deletes NOTHING — silent
-#   under `-q batch` (measured on-box 2026-08-02; docs/WIFI-DUAL-BAND.md §9 item 6).
-#   It has never wiped 09_'s radio1 seed. Do NOT "repair" it into working
-#   per-section deletes: 09_ self-deletes after seeding, so a real radio1 wipe here
-#   would ship a 5 GHz-only box on a virgin overlay. To reset only this radio:
-#   delete wireless.radio0 / delete wireless.default_radio0.
+# Reset only the 5 GHz sections owned by this script.  Older revisions used the
+# invalid package-only `delete wireless` as a deliberate no-op so radio1 would
+# survive.  Depending on an error as control flow is fragile; explicit radio0
+# deletes preserve the 2.4 GHz radio1 seed while making the intent unambiguous.
 uci -q batch <<'EOF'
-delete wireless
+delete wireless.radio0
+delete wireless.default_radio0
 set wireless.radio0='wifi-device'
 set wireless.radio0.type='mac80211'
 set wireless.radio0.hwmode='11a'
